Track 1: Black Wattle Swallows the Reel
- Story & Context: Late one winter evening in a drafty Merri Creek basement in Northcote, sound archivist Maya Sterling carefully threaded the rusted time-capsule reel onto an uncalibrated Studer deck, the sharp scent of damp silt and vinegar-sour tape oxide filling the cold air. Before she could adjust the pitch control, the warped plastic spool seized against the guide pin, snapping the deck's secondary belt and locking the decayed eucalyptus drone into an accidental, hypnotic 78 BPM groove. Tariq Finch grabbed a dangling Shure 58 to ride the stuttering flutter before the tape snapped completely, catching lightning in a bottle with the infectious refrain: "we trade the city pavement for the mud below, spooling the silt back through the teeth of time." The resulting one-take capture proved that the capsule's rot wasn't an audio flaw, but a rhythmic blueprint.
